### What does this question ask?

The interviewer wants the second-smallest value from a list. This mirrors the second-largest question but with ascending order.

### How do you find the second-smallest number?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.*;

public class SecondSmallest {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Integer> fees = List.of(
            500, 1000, 250, 500, 750, 250);

        Optional<Integer> secondSmallest = fees.stream()
            .distinct()
            .sorted()
            .skip(1)
            .findFirst();

        System.out.println("Second-smallest fee: Rs."
            + secondSmallest.map(String::valueOf).orElse("N/A"));
    }
}
```

```text
Second-smallest fee: Rs.500
```

### How is this different from the second-largest?

| Aspect | Second-largest | Second-smallest |
|---|---|---|
| Sort order | reverseOrder() (descending) | naturalOrder() (ascending) |
| Skips | Maximum value | Minimum value |
| Returns | Runner-up from top | Runner-up from bottom |

The pattern is identical; only the sort direction changes.

### Why does distinct matter here?

```java
List<Integer> fees = List.of(250, 250, 500);

// WRONG: without distinct, second element is still 250
Optional<Integer> wrong = fees.stream()
    .sorted().skip(1).findFirst();
System.out.println("Without distinct: " + wrong.get());  // 250!

// CORRECT: distinct collapses duplicate amounts
Optional<Integer> correct = fees.stream()
    .distinct().sorted().skip(1).findFirst();
System.out.println("With distinct: " + correct.get());  // 500
```

### What about negative numbers?

```java
List<Integer> mixed = List.of(-10, 5, -10, 20, -5);

Optional<Integer> result = mixed.stream()
    .distinct()
    .sorted()
    .skip(1)
    .findFirst();

System.out.println(result.get());  // -5
```

Natural ordering handles negative numbers correctly: -10 < -5 < 5 < 20.

### What are the edge cases?

```java
// All same: no second-smallest
Optional<Integer> same = List.of(100, 100).stream()
    .distinct().sorted().skip(1).findFirst();
System.out.println(same.isPresent());  // false

// Single element: no second
Optional<Integer> single = List.of(100).stream()
    .distinct().sorted().skip(1).findFirst();
System.out.println(single.isPresent());  // false

// Empty list
Optional<Integer> empty = List.<Integer>of().stream()
    .distinct().sorted().skip(1).findFirst();
System.out.println(empty.isPresent());  // false
```

### What about the O(n) alternative?

```java
int min = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
int secondMin = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
for (int n : fees) {
    if (n < min) {
        secondMin = min;
        min = n;
    } else if (n < secondMin && n != min) {
        secondMin = n;
    }
}
```

This single-pass approach is O(n), while the stream sort is O(n log n).

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake: using sorted(reverseOrder()) when you want the second-smallest.

```java
// WRONG: descending gives second-largest, not second-smallest
Optional<Integer> wrong = fees.stream()
    .distinct()
    .sorted(Comparator.reverseOrder())
    .skip(1)
    .findFirst();  // second-LARGEST

// CORRECT: ascending (default) for second-smallest
Optional<Integer> correct = fees.stream()
    .distinct()
    .sorted()  // ascending
    .skip(1)
    .findFirst();
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "How do you find both second-smallest and second-largest?" Combine both patterns or use a single sort with skip from both ends.
- "How do you find the Nth-smallest?" Change skip(1) to skip(n-1).
- "How do you find the median?" Sort, then take the middle element(s).
